 },
 "levels_long": {
  "s": "Tables of values, roots, intercept and turning point from a graph.",
  "e": "Roots and turning points by algebra, sketching, symmetry, and tampered evidence.",
  "m": "Finding the equation, solving with a line, and the discriminant."
 },
 "briefing": [
  "The graph of <i>y</i> = <i>a</i><i>x</i>² + <i>b</i><i>x</i> + <i>c</i> is a U shape (a ∩ if <i>a</i> is negative). The <b>roots</b> are where it crosses the <i>x</i>-axis: solve <i>y</i> = 0. The <b>y-intercept</b> is (0, <i>c</i>).",
  "The <b>turning point</b> sits on the line of symmetry, halfway between the roots. Completing the square gives it directly: <i>y</i> = (<i>x</i> − <i>p</i>)² + <i>q</i> has turning point (<i>p</i>, <i>q</i>)."
 ],
 "method": [
  "Roots: factorise and solve = 0 (or read where the curve crosses the x-axis).",
  "y-intercept: put x = 0.",
  "Turning point: halfway between the roots, or complete the square.",
  "To solve an equation with a graph, rearrange it so one side is the curve, and draw the other side as a line."
 ],
 "example": {
  "question": "Find the roots, the y-intercept and the turning point of <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>² − 6<i>x</i> + 5.",
  "steps": [
   "Roots: (<i>x</i> − 1)(<i>x</i> − 5) = 0, so <i>x</i> = 1 and <i>x</i> = 5.",
   "y-intercept: when <i>x</i> = 0, <i>y</i> = 5, so (0, 5).",
   "Line of symmetry halfway between the roots: <i>x</i> = 3.",
   "Turning point: <i>y</i> = 9 − 18 + 5 = −4, so (3, −4). Check: (<i>x</i> − 3)² − 4 = <i>x</i>² − 6<i>x</i> + 5 ✓"
  ],
  "answer": "Roots 1 and 5, y-intercept (0, 5), turning point (3, −4)"
 },
